# Ageing and Dementia/ Clinical Skill's Session: Dementia and Cognitive Impairment

- **Epidemiology:**
  - Increasing incidence (Alzheimers Disease International publishes World Alzheimer Report)
  - Increasing incidence in Africa or Chinese is simply because not increased recognition or older age, just not broad enough literature search, particularly language-specific (only searching anglophone literature)
  - Estimate provided by global burden of disase (2019 Dementia Forecasting Collaborators Lancet public health 2022)
  - HK Prevalence of very mild and mild dementia in community-dwelling older Chinse people in HK in 2005 (6.2%, of which 2/3 are AD while 1/3 VD, FTD and LBD are not that recognised then) (International Psychogeriatrics 2008)
  - Lam et al (unpublished) - mild NCD 22%, major NCD 7.4% (subgroup analysis continues shows increased risk w/ age \[\>75y\], and those in OAH)
  - Costly disease (in terms of trillion per year)
- **Main drivers:**
  - Better awareness
  - Increasing age
- **Differences in prevelance:**
  - Socioeconomic factors - poorer higher in prevalence due to reduced health consciousness, and reduced access to healthcare (OC of sick leave)
- **Diagnostic criteria** (need to know that ICD-10 based on evidence on the 1980s as mentions a decline in memory; now not a must):
  - Now review ICD-10 and evolution to ICD-11
  - Now cognitive decline as 2 of 6 core cognitive functions (DSM-5):
    - Complex attention
    - Executive function
    - Learning and memory
    - Language
    - Perceptual motor (e.g. driving skills)
    - Social cognition (e.g. TOM, reading emotions)
  - DSM-5 defines NCD (neurocognitive disorder) requires specifier:
    - Due to AD
    - Due to FTD
    - Due to LBD
    - Due to vascular
    - TBI
    - Substance abuse
    - Many more in DSM-5
- **NIA-AA criteria for AD** (2011):
  - Biomarkers of brain amyloid-beta protein deposition (CSF A-beta-42 or positive PET amyloid imaging)
  - Biomarker of neuronal degeneration or injury (increased CSF total, phopshorylate dtau, reduced FDG uptake by temporoparietal cortex, disproportionate atrophy in medial, basal, and lateral temporal and medial parietal lobe on MRI)
  - Dynamic biomarker evolution of AD (Jack Cr Lancet Neuro 2013; Alzheimers Dementia 2024), no definite time yet
  - N Engl J Med Jia et al specifically describes time to clinical Sx/ diagnosis (late Dx underlies possibility why drug trials are all -ve at phase 3 or earlier)
- **DSM-5 for AD borrows from NIA-AA:**
  - Mild vs major NCD based on number of domains present
  - Temporal evolution (steadily decline etc)
  - Incorporates evidence of genetics (APP, PSEN1, PSEN2) for "probable AD"
- **AD natural progression is less variable and more predictable**
- **Now AD Dx uses:**
  - Biomarkers
  - Blood-based biomarkers for AD (Lancet Neuro 2022) in slides p34) now used in memory clinic
  - Plasma pTau217 used in QMH memory clinic and actually published (joint study for Geri, psychi and pathology)
- **Vascular dementia:**
  - 15% of dementia
  - More subcortical vascular disease rather than large cortical infarcts
  - Competing criteria - DSM and ADDTC
  - <u>Clinical features</u> - much more variable in which progression is very unpredictable:
    - Stepwise or fluctuating decline, gradual onset or rapid develop followed by relative stability
    - However, most commonly speed of information processing and complex attention is most severely affected
    - Frontal-executive function, memory and language is affected
    - Usually presents w/ depression and apathy (presents more like depression than AD)
  - <u>Causes</u> (O'Brien et al Lancet series on VD 2015) - define very different subtypes
  - Only role of neuroimaging (MRI, CT, transcrranial doppler), no clinically used CSF/ serum biomarkers
  - <u>DSM-5 criteria</u>:
    - Complex attention, executive speed, and frono-temporal key features
- **LBD** ( cf PDD, 12 mo rule):
  - 10-15% of dementia
  - Consortium criteria
  - Pathology:
    - Alpha-synuclein neuronal inclusion bodies
    - Neurofibrilliary tangles
    - Underlying pathology is often mixed amyloid and LB depending on LBD or PDD
  - Clinical features - very fluctuating (can be very strong or weak):
    - Pronounced variations in attention and alertness
    - Very well formed visual hallucinations (much more vivid than those seen in AD and VD)
    - Spontaneous features of parkinsonism with onset subsequents to the development of cognitive decline
    - REM-sleep behavioural disorder (e.g. loss of muscle atonia during REM phase results in actual movement during REM sleep; i.e. we never move in vivid dreams because of normal muscle atonia)
    - Neuroleptics often many S/E in these patients
  - Walker et al Lancet 2015 shows biomarker and clinical temporal evolution similar to Jack for AD
  - Read DSM-5 criteria, know core features:
  - Fourth consensus criteria of DLB consortium (McKeith et al Nuerology 2017):
    - Core features include fluctuating, visual hallucinations, REM sleep behaviour and other parkinsonism features
    - Additional supportive features
    - Indicative biomarkers:
      - BG w/ reduced dopamine transporter uptake by SPECT or PET
      - Abnormallow uptake of iodine-MIBG myocardial scintigraphy (quantification of postganglionic sympathetic cardiac innervation)
      - Polysonographic confirmation of REM sleep without atonia
    - Supportive biomarkers:
      - r/o AD –\> relative preservation of medial temporal lobe structures (e.g. hippocampus)
      - Generalised low uptake on SPECT/ PET
- **Frontotemporal dementia:**
  - 3rd most common form of degenerative dementia, consider for early onset dementia
  - 3 clinical variants - but will eventually converge in late stage:
    - Behavioural
    - 2 language (semantic variant PPA vs non-fluent variant PPA)
  - Behavioural - disinhibition (see slides for details), think **new** (all along more suggestive of personality) criminal behaviours
    - Other features include apathy (flattened affect and ?reduced emotional resonance) and loss of sympathy and empathy
    - Sterotypical, compulsive or preservative
    - Hyperorality or dietary changes, w/ deficits in executive tasks, w/ visuospatial skills fairly preserved initially
  - Primary progressive aphasia - most commonly a/w FTD but can seen in AD:
    - Progressive insidious decline in linguistic skills
    - Language dysfunction main Sx, w/ minimal bhehavioural disturbances
    - Semantic variant vs non-fluent variant
  - Imaging:
    - Structural MRI and CT to differentiate AD based on anatomical localisation
    - FUnctional studies disproportional hypoperfusion to anterior lesions)
    - NO AMYLOID PLAQUES
    - Many others
  - Pathology:
    - Microtubule-associated protein tau
    - TDP-43
  - Genetics - very strong FHx
  - Biomarkers not too available now
- **Approach to cognitive decline:**
  - Hx
  - P*E (physical* mental)
  - Ix
- **Salient points of Hx** - patient and informant (patients prone to confabulation):
  - 
- **Ix:**
  - CBC, LRFT, Ca, Glu, TFT, B12/ folate
  - VDRL
  - Neuroimaging
  - Special Ix - EEG, LP, biomarkers

See slides tagent on MMSE:

- Created principally for assylums to document cognitive changes for schizophrenics
- Cut-off based on C-MMSE study 1998 w/ reference to education level (technically, should adjust for age but no one has validated this before)
- Limitations:
  - Copyright (they guy of retired; and sold it to the a company) - sls, previously still used without paying, but one day PT COC once wrote to legal issue, then everyone changed to MoCA
  - Include test on executive function

See slides on MoCA:

- Includes executive function which is lacking on MMSE

- FAS (letter fluency) test changed category fluency test

- Validation of chinese MOCA in different populations (one cutoff never fits all, stratified by age and education, but this study performed on stroke patients)

- **Mx:**

  - <u>Pharm</u> - for cognitive and mood

- **Pharm for cognitive function:**

  - AChEi (now expanded to all including severe dementia) and NMDA antagonist (moderate and severe dementia only)

- **AChEi and NMDA antagonist:**

  - Very small effective size
  - Non disease-modifying, Likely symptomatic in early phase (Donepazole open label followup confirms this)
  - Likely better effect with more severe disorders

- **Aducanuab:**

  - Anti-amyloid
  - Shows mild halting disease progression
  - Subsequently replaced bby lecanemab infusion (Van Dyck 2023)
  - Only for mild cases
  - Judacious use in patients with risk factors such as APOE4 homozygosity and amyloid angiopathy and anticoagulant
  - Difficult to implent due to come in for infusion and often require serial imaging to demonstrate improvement
  - New with donanemab (TRAILBLAZER trial)

- **New disease targeting therapies** - biologics and small molecules (for early disease n=only)

- **Pharmacological therapy for behaviour and psychological Sx:**

  - Antipsychotic
  - Antidepressants
  - Other for cognition and disease modifying therapy

- **Non-pharmacological:**

  - Cognitive stimulation (Wood 2012 Cochrane review)
  - Training and others
  - Exercise? bit controversial
  - Nutritional intervention
  - Finger Study (multimodal intereventions combining all, esp vascular risk monitoring and diet)

# Personality Disorder

- Why would someome act like that over and over again?
- Stalhl psychopharmacology
- Counter-transference - Freud viewed as obstacle to therapy but now considered a vital clinical tool, therapists unconscious emotional reactions, feelings, and thoughts towards a patient, often triggered by patients own transferred emotions or past experiences
- **LOs:**
  - "Normal personality" vs personality disorder
  - 10-specific PDs: causes and Tx
  - Comorbidities
- **What is a personality:**
  - <u>Definitions</u>:
    - Stuart - set of deeply, ingrained enduring pattern of thinking feeling and bhaviour
    - Boyd - largely outside of the awareness
    - Freud - the ego isn't master in its own house
  - PD is ego-syntonic (cf other axis I disorders which may be ego-dystonic)
  - Personality traits-personality disorder continuum (see that slide) - the difference being functional impairment
- **DSM-5 principles of diagnosis of PD:**
  - A and B) - enduring pattern of inner experience and markedly from expecation of culture, affecting 2/4 domains:
    - Cognition (thinking, distored patterns)
    - Affectivity (feeling, problematic responses)
    - Impulse control (over- or under-regulated impulse control)
    - Interpersonal functioning (problematic relationships)
  - C\) - Pervasive and inflexible
  - D\) - Stable and long duration, onset traced back at least to adolescence or early adulthood
  - E\) - stable over time and leads to <u>distress or impairment in functionng</u>
  - F\) - Not better explained by another mental diosrder, substance, medication, or another medical condition
- **Cf ICD-11:**
  - Dimensional approach characterised by problems in functioning of aspects of self(e.g. identity, self-worth, accuracy of self-view, self-distortion) and/or interpersonal relationships (e.g. intimacy, empathy, conflict resolution) instead of strict categorisation in DSM-5
  - May manifest as dominant traits:
    - Negative affectivity
    - Detachment
    - Dissociality
    - Disinhibition
    - Anankastia
- **Dx:**
  - Thorough personal and social Hx
  - MSE
  - Collateral information esp. when individual denies or is unaware of their maladaptive traits
  - Caution if active mental disorders
  - R/o other medical conditions or presence of other comorbid conditions
  - Multiple admisions, interviews over space and time
- **10 types of personality disorders by DSM-V conventions:**
  - <u>Clusters A</u> - generally more eccentric (weird), paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal
  - <u>Cluster B</u> - dramatic (wild), generally comorbid, eating disorder (as impulsive, over-control eating to gain balance), antisocial, borderline, histrionic, narcicisstic
  - <u>Cluster C</u> - fearful (worry), avoidant, dependent, obsessive-compulsive
- **Paranoid personality disorder:**
  - <u>Core features</u> - distrust or suspiciousness (4/7):
    - Unjustified suspicious of being harmed deceived or exploited
    - Unwarranted doubts about the loyalty or trustworthiness of friends or associates
    - Reluctance to confide in others because of suspicious
    - Tendancy to read hidden meanings into benign actions
    - Bears grudges for perceived wrongs
    - Angry reactions to perceived attacks on echaracter or reputation
    - Unwarranted suspiciousness of the fidelity of the partner
- **Schizoid personality disorder:**
  - M \> F
  - <u>Core features</u> - feeatures of aloofness and flat affect (4/7):
    - Lack of desire for or enjoyment of close relationship
    - Prefers solitude to companionship (not afraid of companionship, just lack of desire, cf avoidant)
    - Little intrest in sex
    - Few or no pleasurable activities
    - Lack of friends
    - Indifference to praise or critism
    - Flat affect, emotional detachment
  - <u>Clinical features</u>:
    - Almost pervasively detached socially out of personal preference
    - Very limited emotions in interpersonal situations
    - a*w prevalence of schizophrenia* schizotypal personality disorders in relatives
  - <u>DDx</u>:
    - ASD
    - Delusional disorder, schiophrenia
    - Avoidant personality disorder (desires but fearful)
  - <u>Mx</u>:
    - Usually forced to seek professional help
    - Social skills (e.g. empathy), value of interpersonal relationships
- **Schizotypal personality disorder:**
  - 75% M
  - Genetic association w/ schizophrenia, may develop into full blown FEP
  - <u>Core features</u> - aloof and flat affect but more features related to schizophrenia (5/9):
    - (overvalued) ideas of reference (not to delusional intensity)
    - Odd beliefs or magical thinking that influences behaviour and is inconsistent with subcultural norms
    - Unusual perceptual experiences (but not to degree of hallucinations)
    - Odd thinking and speech
    - Suspicious or paranoid ideations
    - Inappropriate or constricted affect
    - Behaviour or appearence that is odd, eccentric, or perculiar
    - Lack of close friends or confidant other than first-degree relatives
    - Excessive social anxiety that tends to be a/w paranoid fears)
- **Borderline peresonality disorder:**
  - Most common personality disorder
  - Completed suicide rate ~10% due to impulsivity, although might be just "attention seeking"
  - <u>Core features</u> - over-atachment, ?tendancy of emotional bondage driven by poor self image (5/9):
    - Frantic efforts to avoid abandonment
    - Unstable interpersonal relationship in which others are either idealized or devalued (often very extreme when viewing people)
    - Unstable sense of self
    - Self-harm, impulsive behaviour in lat least two areas, spending, sex, substance, driving, eating
    - Recurrent suicidal behaviour, gestures or self-injurious behavior
    - Chronic feelings of emptiness (?lack of life meaning hence meaning derived from "relationships")
    - Recurrent bouts of intense or poorly controlled anger
    - During stress a tendancy to experience transient paranoid thoughts and dissociative Sx (e.g. dissociative amnesia unable to recall what is said when very angry)
  - <u>General features</u>:
    - Patterns of unstable mood and relationships
    - Impulsivity, fear of abandoment driven by a very poor self image
    - Mutilation and suicidal gestures are common
    - Psychiatric comorbidities common
  - <u>Comorbid</u> - PTSD, depression, substance abuse, eating disorders, anorexia nervosa
  - <u>Mx</u> - Tx comorbidities, SSRIs offer short-term relief, dialectic behaviour therapy (DBT) to Tx the personality, principally by finding "life-meaning"
- **Antisocial personality disorder** - requires \> 18y and evidence of conduct disorder \< 15y:
  - 75% M
  - <u>Core features</u> - induce pain on others/ disregard rights of others (cf on oneself in BPD) (3/7):
    - Repeated law breaking
    - Deceitful and lying
    - Lack of remorse
    - Impulsivity
    - Irritability and aggressiveness
    - Reckless disregard for own safety
    - Irresponsibility
  - Relationship between psychopathy and APD, ASPD, conduction disorder and early behaviour problems
  - Usually come from families w/ criminal/ violent behavioru, or families with inconsistent parental discipline and support
- **Narcissistic personality disorder:**
  - <u>Core features</u> - grandiosity and inflated self-esteem (5/8):
    - Grandiose view of one's importance
    - Preoccupation with ones success, brilliance, beauty
    - Belief that one is special and can be understood only by other high status people
    - Extreme need for admiration
    - Tendency to exploit others
    - Lack of empathy
    - Envious of others
    - Arrogant behaviour or attitudes
  - <u>Major DDx</u>:
    - Grandiose delusions in manic episodes (or other delusional disorders)
- **Histrionic personality disorder:**
  - <u>Core features</u> - excessive emotionality and attention seeking (5/8):
    - Strong need to be center of attention
    - Inappropriate sexually seductive behaviour
    - Rapidly shifting expression of emotions
    - Physical appearace to draw attention to self
    - Speech is impressionistic and lacking detail
    - Theatrical emotional expression
    - Overly suggestible
    - Misreads relationships as more intimate than they are
  - <u>Major DDx</u>:
    - Borderline
    - Narcicistic
- **Avoidant personality disorder:**
  - <u>Core features</u> - social inhibition out of feeling of inadequacy or hypersensitivity to criticism (4/7):
    - Avoidance of occupational activities that involves significant interpersonal contact because of fears of criticism
    - Unwilling to get involved with people unless certain of being liked
    - Restrained in intimate relationships because of the fear of being shamed or ridiculed
    - preoccupation with being criticized or rejected
    - Inhibited in new personal situations
    - Views self as socially inept or inferior
    - Usually reluctant to try new activities
  - <u>General features</u>:
    - Hypersensitvity to critism
    - Highly avoidant to most interpersonal relationships
    - Anxious and fearful of rejection
  - <u>Causes</u> - ?difficult temperament
  - <u>DDx</u> - schizoid/ schizotypal (this one lacks desire), vs introversion (usually will not deny life defining…), social anxiety disorder
- **Dependent personality disorder:**
  - <u>Core features</u> - needs to be taken care of (5/8):
    - Difficulty making decisions w/o excessive advice and reassurance from others
    - Need for other to take responsibility for most major areas of life
    - Difficutlty disagreeing with others for fear of losing the support
    - Difficulty doing things on own because lack of self confidence
    - Doing unpleasant things as a way to obtain the approval or support of others
    - Feeling of helplessness when alone because of lack of confidence in ability to handle things w/o others
    - Urgently seeking new relationship when one ends
    - Preoccupation w/ feares on how to take care of self
  - <u>DDx</u> - borderline (also bad relationship, where dependence just find new)
- **OC personality disorders:**
  - <u>Not OCD</u> - egodystonia vs egosyntonia
  - <u>Core features</u> - intense need for order and control (4/8), may be a bit perfectionalist:
    - Pre-occulpation w/ rules, detail and organisation to the point the activity is lost
    - Extreme perfectionism interfers w/ task completion
    - Excssive devotion to work to the exclusion of leisure and friendships
    - Inflexible about morals and values
    - Difficulty discarding worthless items
    - Reluctance to delegate unless other conforms to one's standard
    - Miserliness
    - Rigidity and stubborness

# Clinical Skills Session: Bipolar Affective Disorder

- **Salient points of Hx in bipolar disorder:**
  - Sociodemographic basics
  - HPI:
    - Reason of consultation
    - Onset (age of onset usually late adolescence and early twenties)
      - First presentation may not be manic but a subclinical episodes, need to clarify this after the first episode
      - Especially if presenting in 40s, consider relapse and clarifying previous episodes
    - General course of illness –\> progression and course, e.g. number of episodes, worst episode, hospitalisation, how long is hospitalisation if present, prior ECTs, mood stablisers, other drugs (e.g. antidepressants), inter-episodic full remission of Sx and functioning
    - Past episode of antidepressant mania
    - Inter-episodic full remission of Sx and functioning requires clarifying this, more likely bipolar vs schizoaffective/ schizophrenia (DDx of the two forms of psychotic disorders)
  - Symptom clarification:
    - Depressive Sx - anhedonia, low mood, negative thoughts, and ?fatigue
    - Manic Sx - mood eleation, gradiosity and increased energy levels
  - Reason of consultations:
    - Sx
    - Establish pathway to care, depends on mental health literacy, support system, involuntary admissions etc.
  - Past psychiatric Hx (prior counselling)
  - PMH:
    - Drug use affecting mood stabiliser and antipsychotic
    - Substance abuse
    - Medical conditions that can mimick depressive and manic episodes (e.g. thyroid, steroid use/ Cushing's, post-stroke, post-partum, cancer, tuberculosis)
  - FHx:
    - Schizophrenia and depression, bipolar (likely shared genetic substrate w/ schizophrenia or other psychosis)
    - Commited suicide
    - Note family support
  - Personal Hx:
    - Poor childhood events, traumatic episodes –\> affects emotional regulation and affects coping
    - Education level important as baseline of cognitive function, and can modify presentation of mood Sx
    - Employment record (evaluation of best premorbid functioning, any sudden give up jobs)
    - Marital status
  - Personality (usu. important for mood disorders):
    - How risk taking normally
    - How dysthymic
    - What is stress coping
    - Interpersonal Hx
    - Illicit substance use and forensics
  - Risk assessment:
    - Self harm and suicide
    - Violence (verbal or physical, any legality issues)
    - ?Impulsivity (e.g. spending)
- **How to elicit manic Sx:**
  - Core:
    - Mood elation:
      - Onset
      - Description - Flying, floating sensation, uncontrollable etc.
      - Timing - pervasiveness, even during normally tedious times, i.e. environmental factors cannot affect the elation
      - How other describes the patient
    - Grandiosity (N.B. may not be delusional level as not unshakable and persistent w/ contradictory evidence):
      - Sensation - feeling good, special skills, coparison with before
      - Planning and actual action
      - w/ powerful people association
    - Increased energy:
      - Sleep - less sleep and not fatiguable
      - General energy levels - doing many things or planning many things
      - Doing work and disturbing hours during midnight
      - ?Flight of ideas, racing thoughts, which cannot be controlled
      - Other people seem slower to react than u during conversation
      - Poor attention and concentration due to increased energy level
      - Other people reflecting - pressure of speech, people unable to understand
  - Others:
    - Psychotic Sx - delusion and hallucination:
      - Common manic delusions - delusion of love, delusion of reference (neutral signals having salience), percusation, religious, gradiosity etc. (gradiose -\> love -\> reference -\> jealousy -\> percusation -\> religion)
      - Hallucinations - auditory most common, visual (rare, usually organic or intoxication), first rank (10% of manic episode experience first rank Sx)
    - Risky behaviour:
      - Suicide
      - Violence
      - Financial - spending spree, investment
      - Driving
      - Substance abuse
      - Sex
- **How to elicit depressive Sx:**
  - Core:
    - Low mood:
      - Onset
      - Description - sometimes depressive cognition may be described here
      - Timing - pervasiveness, even during otherwise happy times, compare w/ the past
      - How others describe you
    - Anhedonia:
      - Cannot experience happiness and pleasure (in chinese think about it)
      - Basically –\> u know you want to be happy, have you tried anything to make yourself happy, what do u do usually find pleasurable
      - Use hypothetic scenarios, e.g. if problems solved can it make you happy
    - Low energy level:
      - Fatigue
      - Low motivation
      - Resting a lot but waking up not well
      - No planning
      - Bodily
      - Mind - slow thinking, don't want to speak, cannot concentrate or pay attention
  - Others:
    - Biological Sx of depression (e.g. somatic Sx) - predicts positive response to antidepressants, early morning wakening, weight loss, poor appetite, lack of libido, lack of diurnal varaiation of mood, morning depression
    - Personality, childhood adversities, psychosocial stress usually do not respond well to antidepressants
    - Psychotic Sx:
      - Depressive delusions - catastrophic nihlistic, hypochondriac, percusatory, guilt (guilt/ worthlessness –\> reference –\> percusatory –\> catastrophic (with reference to guilt) –\> nihlistic)
      - Hallucinations - psychotic depressions may have olfactory depression (smell like a corspe)
    - Risk - suicide, ?violence for psychotic/ agitated depression (post-partum depression and infanticide)
